The commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 6, verse 57:

When their demand for signs (al-ayat, meaning the evident proofs of truthfulness), sometimes through mercy in the sending down of rivers and treasures and the easing of life, and sometimes through punishment, such as the sky being made to fall upon them in pieces and the like, was something whose timing lay neither in his hand nor with him, he was commanded here to declare to them plainly the separation (al-mubayanah) and to make them despair of compromise (al-mulayanah) for as long as they persisted in flattery (al-mudahanah). He was commanded to inform them of what he possessed of light and of what they were in of blindness, by saying: ﴿Say: Indeed I﴾. He indicated his firm footing in the clear proofs and overwhelming arguments by the particle of elevation (harf al-isti'la'), saying: ﴿am upon a clear proof﴾. That is: the enemy only placates his enemy either because he lacks confidence in victory over him and fears his hostility, or because he is not sure that he is upon the truth. As for me, I am certain of both matters, ﴿from my Lord﴾, that is, the One who has been good to me by sending me after fully unveiling to me the secret of the dominion and the malakut (the unseen realm). ﴿And you﴾ — the situation being that you ﴿have denied Him﴾, that is, my Lord, by rejecting His messenger. So He will certainly take vengeance upon you.

When this was said, it was supposed that the tongue of their situation said: "Then bring us this proof!" So he said: Indeed my Lord has complete power, so He does not fear missing out, and therefore He does not hasten. As for me, I am a servant, ﴿and I do not have﴾, that is, within my power and capacity, ﴿what you seek to hasten﴾, that is, in your saying "Rain down upon us stones from the sky" and the like, until I judge among you by what human nature requires of haste. ﴿The judgment is not﴾, that is, there is no judgment in anything whatsoever, this and other than it, ﴿except for Allah﴾, that is, the One to whom the entire affair belongs, so there is no equal to Him. Then he resumed his speech, clarifying that He, glorified is He, brings the matter at the time He has set for it, in the manner most fitting to Him, without any ability for anyone other than Him to bring it forward or delay it. So he said: ﴿He relates﴾, that is, He explains and executes with advancement and postponement. This is the meaning of the reading of the two Harams (Ibn Kathir and Nafi', or the Makki and Madani readers) and 'Asim, "yaqussu" (meaning: He cuts off the judgment or the narration). ﴿The truth﴾ and makes it manifest, distinguishing it from falsehood and making it clear, so that whoever has been decreed to happiness follows it, and whoever has been decreed to wretchedness turns away from it. ﴿And He is the best of deciders﴾, because when He wills that, He leaves no ambiguity for the one whom He wills to guide, and He places in that clear evidence a cause of misguidance for the one whom He wills to lead astray;

Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 6, verse 63:

When He made Himself known through His acts and affairs, until His oneness became clear and His singleness was established, He reminded them of their state, in affirming His monotheism at times of hardship, and of their turning away from that at the moment of deliverance from it. They were like one who asked a person for something and confirmed a covenant with him to be grateful; then when that person did him good by giving him his request, he broke his pledge and went to extremes in disbelief. This, in their view, is among the ugliest of vices that cannot be described. So He said: ﴿Say﴾, that is: to these people who claim the excellence of their deeds, ﴿"Who delivers you"﴾, that is: [delivers you] from many great things, ﴿"from the darknesses of the land and the sea"﴾, that is: where there is no guidance for you by a star, a mountain, or other such things. Or He expressed by "darknesses" the calamities whose severity reaches the point that their sufferer is as if he were in the deepest darkness, such that he finds no way out by any means of stratagem. ﴿"You call upon Him"﴾, that is: in sincere devotion to Him, in monotheism, and turning away from every association and every partner, because worldly interests vanish when terror overwhelms and seizes the heart's every faculty, so nothing remains except the sound natural disposition (fitrah). The Imam ʿAbd al-Haqq al-Ishbili said in his book (al-Wai): ﴿"humbly"﴾, that is: displaying supplication (ḍarāʿah), which is extreme poverty, and its reality is humility. And his saying "in secret" means: you conceal in your souls the same as what you display. Shimr said: it is said, "ḍaraʿa lahu," and he is "ḍāriʿ," clearly supplicating; these are "ḍuraʿ" people, that is, abased, and they are "ḍaraʿah," that is, supplicants. Supplication to God is humbling oneself before Him and submission. And when a man is weak in body, one says: he is "ḍāriʿ al-jism," clearly weakened in body, and in abasement, "bayyin al-ḍarāʿah." End.

When He had made clear their description at the time of supplication, He made clear their words at that moment, so He said: ﴿"If You deliver us from this, we will surely be among the thankful"﴾. They emphasized, specified and made the clarification as clear as possible, ﴿"we will surely be among the thankful"﴾, that is: those who excel in gratitude;
